<exercises xml:id="matlab-desktop-coding-exercises">
  <title>💻 Coding Exercises</title>

  <exercisegroup>
    <title>Foundational</title>

    <exercise label="desktop-ex-explore-panels0">
      <title>Explore Desktop Panels</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          These exercises guide you through interacting with each major panel of the MATLAB Desktop.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task label="desktop-ex-explore-panels-a0">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Open MATLAB and identify the four main panels: Files, Command Window, Workspace, and Editor. In the Command Window, type the following and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>:
          </p>
          <program language="matlab">
            x = 10
          </program>
          <p>
            Verify that <pf>x</pf> appears in the Workspace panel with value <c>10</c>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <p>
            After typing <pf>x = 10</pf> and pressing <kbd>Enter</kbd>, the Command Window shows <c>x = 10</c> and the Workspace panel updates to list <pf>x</pf> with value <c>10</c>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task label="desktop-ex-explore-panels-b0">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In the Command Window, type <pf>clear</pf> and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. What happens to the Workspace panel?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <p>
            The Workspace panel becomes empty. The <pf>clear</pf> command removes all variables from memory.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task label="desktop-ex-explore-panels-c0">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Open the Editor panel (go to <q>Home</q> tab, click <q>New Script</q>). Type the following in the Editor, save it as <c>myFirstScript.m</c>, and run it:
          </p>
          <program language="matlab">
            a = 3;
            b = 4;
            c = sqrt(a^2 + b^2)
          </program>
          <p>
            Confirm that <pf>c</pf> appears in the Workspace with the correct value and that the output is shown in the Command Window.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <p>
            Running the script produces <c>c = 5</c> in the Command Window and adds <pf>a</pf>, <pf>b</pf>, and <pf>c</pf> to the Workspace. This illustrates how the Editor and Command Window work together.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

    </exercise>
  </exercisegroup>

  <exercisegroup>
    <title>Extended</title>

    <exercise label="desktop-ex-layout-customization">
      <title>Customize and Restore the Layout</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Experiment with the MATLAB Desktop layout:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Hide the Workspace panel by clicking its close button.</li>
          <li>Drag the Command Window to a different position on screen.</li>
          <li>Restore the default layout using the <q>Home</q> tab, <q>Layout</q> dropdown, and selecting <q>Default</q>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Write a brief description (as a MATLAB comment) of what changed when you restored the default layout.
        </p>
        <program language="matlab">
          % Description of what happened when the default layout was restored:
          %
        </program>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
        <p>
          Restoring the default layout resets all panels to their original positions and makes hidden panels visible again. This is useful if the desktop becomes disorganized during customization.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercisegroup>
  
  <exercise label="desktop-activity-match-panels0">
    <title>Match the Panel to Its Purpose</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        For each description, identify the MATLAB Desktop panel it refers to. Type your answer in the Sage cell.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task label="desktop-activity-match-1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          You notice a variable called <pf>totalCost</pf> was accidentally left over from a previous session. In which panel would you see this variable listed?
        </p>
        <sage language="octave">
          <input>
            % Type the name of the panel here as a comment, then evaluate.
            % ==== Type your answer here ====
            % Panel:
          </input>
        </sage>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
        <p>
          The <term>Workspace</term> panel. It lists all variables currently stored in memory, including leftover ones from previous commands.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task label="desktop-activity-match-2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          You want to write a 20-line program that computes several values and saves the result to a file. Which panel do you use?
        </p>
        <sage language="octave">
          <input>
            % Type the name of the panel here as a comment, then evaluate.
            % ==== Type your answer here ====
            % Panel:
          </input>
        </sage>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
        <p>
          The <term>Editor</term>. It is designed for writing, editing, and saving longer programs as script or function files.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task label="desktop-activity-match-3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          You want to quickly test whether <pf>sqrt(144)</pf> returns <c>12</c>. Which panel do you use?
        </p>
        <sage language="octave">
          <input>
            % Type the name of the panel here as a comment, then try the command below.
            % ==== Type your answer here ====
            % Panel:

            sqrt(144)
          </input>
        </sage>
        <p>
          When done, evaluate and confirm the result is <c>ans = 12</c>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
        <p>
          The <term>Command Window</term>. It is the interactive panel for entering and immediately evaluating single commands.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <exercisegroup>
    <title>Variables</title>

  <exercise xml:id="vars-ex-6">
      <title>Volume &amp; Surface Area of a Cylinder</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the MATLAB command window to assign values for the radius and height of a cylinder, then compute its volume and surface area.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="82% 5% 13%" valign="middle">
          <stack>
            <p>
              The volume and surface area of a cylinder is given by:
              <me>V = \pi r^2 h, \qquad S = 2\pi r h + 2\pi r^2</me>
              Use <pf>pi</pf> for <m>\pi</m> and
              use descriptive variable names.
            </p>
            <p>
              Test your work with <m>r = 1.2</m> and <m>h = 3.5</m>.
            </p>
          </stack>
          <p/>
          <image>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
                \def\rad{0.6}
                \def\height{1.3}
                \def\ellh{0.28}

                % cylinder
                \draw[thick] (0,0) ellipse [x radius=\rad, y radius=\ellh];
                \draw[thick] (-\rad,0) -- (-\rad,\height);
                \draw[thick] (\rad,0) -- (\rad,\height);
                \draw[thick] (0,\height) ellipse [x radius=\rad, y radius=\ellh];

                % hidden back half of bottom ellipse
                \draw[dashed] (-\rad,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=\rad,y radius=\ellh];

                % height marker
                \draw[] (0,0) -- (0,\height);
                \node[right, font=\small] at (-0.1,0.5*\height) {$h$};

                % radius marker on top
                \draw[] (0,\height) -- (\rad,\height);
                \node[above] at (0.5*\rad,\height-0.1) {$r$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
        <program language="matlab">
          <code>
            radius = 1.2;
            height = 3.5;
            volume = pi * radius^2 * height
            surfaceArea = 2 * pi * radius * height + 2 * pi * radius^2
          </code>
        </program>
      </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vars-ex-1">
      <title>Speed, Distance, and Time</title>
      <statement>
          <p>
              Write a MATLAB script that stores a car's speed and travel time in variables,
              then computes the distance traveled using <m>d = s \cdot t</m>.
          </p>

          <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
              <col width="11%" halign="right" />
              <col width="15%" halign="left" />
              <col width="28%" halign="center" />
              <col width="46%" halign="left"/>
              <row>
                  <cell>Inputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>speed</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>speed in miles per hour</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>time</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>travel time in hours</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell>Outputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>distance</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>distance traveled in miles</p></cell>
              </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>
              Test with <pf>speed = 60</pf> and <pf>time = 2.5</pf>; the result should be
              <pf>distance = 150</pf>.
          </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <program language="matlab">
<code>
speed    = 60;
time     = 2.5;
distance = speed * time
</code>
          </program>
      </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vars-ex-2">
      <title>Celsius to Fahrenheit</title>
      <statement>
          <p>
              Convert a temperature from Celsius to Fahrenheit using the formula
              <m>F = \tfrac{9}{5}C + 32</m>.
          </p>

          <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
              <col width="11%" halign="right" />
              <col width="15%" halign="left" />
              <col width="28%" halign="center" />
              <col width="46%" halign="left"/>
              <row>
                  <cell>Inputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>tempC</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>temperature in degrees Celsius</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell>Outputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>tempF</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>temperature in degrees Fahrenheit</p></cell>
              </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>
              Test with <pf>tempC = 100</pf>; the result should be <pf>tempF = 212</pf>.
          </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <program language="matlab">
<code>
tempC = 100;
tempF = (9/5) * tempC + 32
</code>
          </program>
      </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vars-ex-3">
      <title>Compound Interest</title>
      <statement>
          <p>
              Compute the balance after compounding for <m>n</m> years using
              <m>A = P(1 + r)^n</m>, where <m>P</m> is the principal, <m>r</m> is the
              annual interest rate, and <m>n</m> is the number of years.
          </p>

          <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
              <col width="11%" halign="right" />
              <col width="15%" halign="left" />
              <col width="28%" halign="center" />
              <col width="46%" halign="left"/>
              <row>
                  <cell>Inputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>principal</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>initial deposit in dollars</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>rate</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>annual interest rate (e.g., 0.05 for 5%)</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>years</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>number of years</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell>Outputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>balance</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>account balance after the given years</p></cell>
              </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>
              Test with <pf>principal = 1000</pf>, <pf>rate = 0.05</pf>,
              <pf>years = 3</pf>; the result should be approximately
              <pf>balance = 1157.625</pf>.
          </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <program language="matlab">
<code>
principal = 1000;
rate      = 0.05;
years     = 3;
balance   = principal * (1 + rate)^years
</code>
          </program>
      </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vars-ex-4">
      <title>Area and Perimeter of a Rectangle</title>
      <statement>
          <p>
              Given the length and width of a rectangle, compute both its area and
              perimeter using descriptively named variables.
          </p>

          <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
              <col width="11%" halign="right" />
              <col width="15%" halign="left" />
              <col width="28%" halign="center" />
              <col width="46%" halign="left"/>
              <row>
                  <cell>Inputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>rectLen</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>length of the rectangle</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>rectWid</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>width of the rectangle</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                  <cell>Outputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>rectArea</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>area of the rectangle</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>rectPerim</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>perimeter of the rectangle</p></cell>
              </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>
              Test with <pf>rectLen = 5</pf> and <pf>rectWid = 3</pf>; results should be
              <pf>rectArea = 15</pf> and <pf>rectPerim = 16</pf>.
          </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <program language="matlab">
<code>
rectLen   = 5;
rectWid   = 3;
rectArea  = rectLen * rectWid
rectPerim = 2 * (rectLen + rectWid)
</code>
          </program>
      </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vars-ex-5">
      <title>Kinetic Energy</title>
      <statement>
          <p>
              Compute the kinetic energy of a moving object using
              <m>KE = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2</m>.
          </p>

          <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
              <col width="11%" halign="right" />
              <col width="15%" halign="left" />
              <col width="28%" halign="center" />
              <col width="46%" halign="left"/>
              <row>
                  <cell>Inputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>mass</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>mass of the object in kilograms</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>velocity</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>speed of the object in meters per second</p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell>Outputs:</cell>
                  <cell><p><pf>kineticEnergy</pf></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>kinetic energy in joules</p></cell>
              </row>
          </tabular>

          <p>
              Test with <pf>mass = 2</pf> and <pf>velocity = 3</pf>; the result should be
              <pf>kineticEnergy = 9</pf>.
          </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="exercise-solution">
          <program language="matlab">
<code>
mass          = 2;
velocity      = 3;
kineticEnergy = 0.5 * mass * velocity^2
</code>
          </program>
      </solution>
  </exercise>
  </exercisegroup>

  </exercises>